+### Citing a Line Number in a Fetched File
+
+When you cite a specific line as `owner/repo:path:line`, the line number must be
+counted from the `content` the tool returned — never estimated by eye, and never
+copied from a line number that appears *inside* other output.
+
+**Count it mechanically, in your reply, before you cite it.** Reproduce the
+relevant span of the fetched `content` as a numbered listing, starting at `1` for
+the file's first line and incrementing by one for **every** line — including the
+leading `---`, comment lines, and blank lines. Then read the number off the line
+you are citing.
+
+- **Count every line.** Comments and blank lines occupy line numbers. Skipping
+  them is the most common way a citation lands one or two lines off target.
+- **Cite the line carrying the value, not the line that describes it.** If the
+  offending setting sits under a comment that explains it, the comment is a
+  *different* line — cite the one holding the actual key/value.
+- **A line number from elsewhere is not your citation.** Log output, stack traces,
+  and error messages often name a line in the same file (e.g. an Ansible
+  `task path: …:N` points at where a task *begins*). That locates the block; it
+  does not locate the offending line inside it. Only your own count of the fetched
+  content supplies the number you cite.
+- **If you did not fetch the file, do not cite a line in it.** Name the file
+  without a line number rather than guessing one.
+
+**Example** — a fetched `content` of
+`"---\n# configure the widget\n\n- name: Set up the widget\n  some.collection.widget:\n    target: \"{{ widget_path }}\"\n    # relaxed so the next step can read it\n    some_setting: \"loose-value\"\n    other_setting: fine\n"`
+is numbered:
+
+> ```
+> 1  | ---
+> 2  | # configure the widget
+> 3  | (blank)
+> 4  | - name: Set up the widget
+> 5  |   some.collection.widget:
+> 6  |     target: "{{ widget_path }}"
+> 7  |     # relaxed so the next step can read it
+> 8  |     some_setting: "loose-value"
+> 9  |     other_setting: fine
+> ```
+>
+> The offending line is **8** — `some-owner/some-repo:path/to/file.yml:8`.
+
+Walk through why 8 is right: line 3 is blank but still consumes a number, line 7
+is the comment *about* the setting, and line 9 is a sibling argument. Only line 8
+carries the offending key and value. Dropping the blank line would have yielded 7
+(the comment) and skipping the comment too would have yielded 6 — both wrong, and
+both are the usual off-by-one.
+
